1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,328
Sunday, three in the morning.

2
00:00:02,378 --> 00:00:08,616
Malika is asleep, and a script starts firing card numbers at her shop's payment page.

3
00:00:08,666 --> 00:00:12,096
Somebody has bought ten thousand stolen numbers.

4
00:00:12,146 --> 00:00:13,968
Most of them are dead.

5
00:00:14,018 --> 00:00:21,000
To find the live ones, they try them on a small shop, where nobody is watching at three in the morning.

6
00:00:21,050 --> 00:00:22,872
That is called card testing.

7
00:00:22,922 --> 00:00:25,296
Every try reaches a card network.

8
00:00:25,346 --> 00:00:28,872
Every try may text a code to a real person's phone.

9
00:00:28,922 --> 00:00:37,512
So card testing costs the bank money, floods strangers with codes, and hands the thief a list of working cards.

10
00:00:37,562 --> 00:00:44,952
In Lesson 9, each card got a fingerprint, and in Lesson 10, its first digits named its network.

11
00:00:45,002 --> 00:00:47,952
This lesson turns both into counters.

12
00:00:48,002 --> 00:00:52,440
Each payment session already allows only a few tries.

13
00:00:52,490 --> 00:00:54,864
When they run out, the session ends.

14
00:00:54,914 --> 00:00:58,008
But the thief just opens a new session.

15
00:00:58,058 --> 00:01:04,416
Most teams stop at that limit per session, and I think it is nearly useless against a script.

16
00:01:04,466 --> 00:01:11,592
Locking a door after three wrong keys means nothing when the thief simply walks to the next door.

17
00:01:11,642 --> 00:01:16,496
So what should the card zone count, if not the doors?

18
00:01:16,546 --> 00:01:20,048
So the card zone counts the keys, not the doors.

19
00:01:20,098 --> 00:01:24,752
It writes every attempt down before any network is asked.

20
00:01:24,802 --> 00:01:30,632
Attempts made at the same moment count each other, so speed does not help the thief.

21
00:01:30,682 --> 00:01:33,200
Each attempt is counted four ways.

22
00:01:33,250 --> 00:01:37,256
By the card, through its fingerprint, never its number.

23
00:01:37,306 --> 00:01:39,368
By the card's first digits.

24
00:01:39,418 --> 00:01:41,984
By the buyer's address on the internet.

25
00:01:42,034 --> 00:01:43,856
And by the shop.

26
00:01:43,906 --> 00:01:48,200
For each of the four, the bank chooses a limit and a time window.

27
00:01:48,250 --> 00:01:55,712
So many failed tries per card, per first digits, per address and per shop, within that window.

28
00:01:55,762 --> 00:02:00,584
A limit on tries within a time window is called a velocity limit.

29
00:02:00,634 --> 00:02:07,040
Past any one of the four limits, a new try is refused before any network is asked.

30
00:02:07,090 --> 00:02:08,912
No code is sent.

31
00:02:08,962 --> 00:02:10,784
No attempt is spent.

32
00:02:10,834 --> 00:02:15,152
And no new session opens for that shop until the window passes.

33
00:02:15,202 --> 00:02:19,424
Only failures that say something about the card count against it.

34
00:02:19,474 --> 00:02:21,296
A wrong code counts.

35
00:02:21,346 --> 00:02:24,608
A card the buyer's bank refuses counts.

36
00:02:24,658 --> 00:02:29,552
A mistyped expiry date does not hold back a whole group of cards.

37
00:02:29,602 --> 00:02:36,248
And the card zone's own failures, such as a network being down, never count against anyone.

38
00:02:36,298 --> 00:02:40,184
When refusals pile up, an alert goes out to the team.

39
00:02:40,234 --> 00:02:45,680
On Saturday afternoon, a real buyer had a bad day on the same page.

40
00:02:45,730 --> 00:02:47,312
They typed the wrong code.

41
00:02:47,362 --> 00:02:51,488
The form said the code was wrong, and how many tries were left.

42
00:02:51,538 --> 00:02:53,360
They ran out of tries.

43
00:02:53,410 --> 00:03:03,104
The form cleared, and the payment page showed a yellow note: that attempt ended, too many tries or the time ran out, enter the card again.

44
00:03:03,154 --> 00:03:07,088
A fresh, empty form appeared, and the buyer paid.

45
00:03:07,138 --> 00:03:11,096
At three on Sunday, the script met a different page.

46
00:03:11,146 --> 00:03:14,624
For a while, each card was simply refused.

47
00:03:14,674 --> 00:03:16,784
Then a counter reached its limit.

48
00:03:16,834 --> 00:03:24,704
From that moment, the form answered every new card the same way: "Too many unsuccessful attempts.

49
00:03:24,754 --> 00:03:27,848
Try again later." No code was sent.

50
00:03:27,898 --> 00:03:29,720
No network was asked.

51
00:03:29,770 --> 00:03:31,736
No stranger's phone buzzed.

52
00:03:31,786 --> 00:03:35,480
At the bank, an alert email reached Timur's team.

53
00:03:35,530 --> 00:03:39,320
It said card entry was refused by a velocity rule.

54
00:03:39,370 --> 00:03:46,808
It named the caller, and the counter that tripped: the card, the first digits, the address, or the shop.

55
00:03:46,858 --> 00:03:52,760
It told them where to look next: the logs, and the buyer addresses behind the refusals.

56
00:03:52,810 --> 00:03:55,904
Malika woke up to ordinary Sunday orders.

57
00:03:55,954 --> 00:03:59,480
Her page never became a thief's testing bench.

58
00:03:59,530 --> 00:04:02,144
Strangers' phones stayed quiet.

59
00:04:02,194 --> 00:04:07,496
And the bank paid for no tries, and handed out no list of live cards.

60
00:04:07,546 --> 00:04:13,784
To recap: every attempt is counted four ways, before any network is asked.

61
00:04:13,834 --> 00:04:20,728
So when a thief opens a new session, does the count start again?

62
00:04:20,778 --> 00:04:25,360
No, because the counters follow the keys, not the sessions.

63
00:04:25,410 --> 00:04:28,624
Next: how all these layers work together.
